        <body>
    <h2>Requesting a Special Education Evaluation in District 21: A Parent's Guide</h2>

    <section>
        <h3>Local Context: Serving Coney Island, Gravesend, Sheepshead Bay & Beyond</h3>
        <p>District 21 in Brooklyn serves a diverse community, including the vibrant neighborhoods of Coney Island, Gravesend, Sheepshead Bay, Brighton Beach, and the southern portion of Midwood. We are committed to ensuring that all children, including those who may require special education services, receive the support they need to thrive. We are particularly sensitive to the needs of our Russian-speaking families and strive to provide bilingual evaluation services when appropriate, adhering to requirements outlined in NYS Part 200 Regulations regarding language access. Families living near our beautiful beach communities have the same access to these services as everyone else.</p>
        <p>This guide provides comprehensive information about requesting a special education evaluation in District 21. Understanding your rights and responsibilities under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State regulations is crucial for advocating effectively for your child.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Initiating the Special Education Evaluation Process: Your Written Request</h3>
        <p>The cornerstone of the special education evaluation process is a <b>written referral</b> to the Committee on Special Education (CSE) Chairperson in District 21. This referral formally initiates the process and triggers the district's legal obligations. The referral must clearly state your concerns regarding your child's academic, social-emotional, or physical development. It's not enough to simply say "my child is struggling." You need to be specific and provide concrete examples. Remember, this is your opportunity to paint a clear picture of your child's needs.</p>

        <p><b>What to include in your written referral:</b></p>
        <ul>
            <li>Your child’s full name, date of birth, address, school, and grade.</li>
            <li>A detailed description of your concerns. For example:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Academic Concerns:</b> "My child is significantly behind grade level in reading comprehension, as evidenced by failing grades on reading assignments and difficulty answering comprehension questions. He requires significant support to decode words and struggles to understand the main idea of texts."</li>
                    <li><b>Social-Emotional Concerns:</b> "My child exhibits frequent outbursts of anger and frustration in the classroom, disrupting learning for themselves and others. He struggles to regulate his emotions and has difficulty interacting appropriately with peers."</li>
                    <li><b>Behavioral Concerns:</b> "My child frequently leaves their seat without permission, talks excessively, and has difficulty following directions. These behaviors significantly interfere with their ability to focus on academic tasks."</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Any documentation that supports your concerns, such as:
                <ul>
                    <li>Report cards showing failing grades or significant academic decline.</li>
                    <li>Doctor's notes or medical evaluations documenting any medical conditions that may be impacting your child's learning.</li>
                    <li>Observations from teachers detailing specific challenges or behaviors observed in the classroom.</li>
                    <li>Examples of your child's work that demonstrate areas of difficulty.</li>
                    <li>Private tutoring reports or evaluations.</li>
                </ul>
            </li>
            <li>Your contact information (phone number and email address).</li>
            <li>A clear statement requesting a special education evaluation.</li>
        </ul>

        <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tip:</b> Keep a copy of your written referral for your records. Send the referral via certified mail with a return receipt requested to ensure proof of delivery. This documentation can be crucial if there are any disputes regarding the date the referral was received.</p>

        <p><b>Contacting the District 21 CSE Chairperson:</b>  You can contact the District 21 CSE Chairperson to understand the process and address any preliminary concerns. They can also provide you with the specific contact details to which you should submit the written referral. Locate contact information on the NYC Department of Education website. Remember to document the date, time, and content of any conversations you have with school personnel.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Important: Understanding the 60-Day Timeline and Parental Consent</h3>
        <p>Once the CSE receives your written referral, the clock starts ticking.  Federal IDEA law (20 U.S.C. § 1414(a)(1)(C)) and New York State regulations (8 NYCRR §200.4(a)(1)(i)) mandate a specific timeline for the evaluation process. The CSE has 60 <u>school</u> days from the date of your referral to complete the evaluation process, including conducting evaluations, holding a CSE meeting, and determining eligibility for special education services. It is crucial to understand this timeline and to actively participate in the process.</p>

        <p><b>Key Steps Within the 60-Day Timeline:</b></p>
        <ol>
            <li><b>Receipt of Referral:</b> The school must acknowledge receipt of your referral in writing.  Document this date carefully, as it marks the beginning of the 60-day period.</li>
            <li><b>Review of Existing Data:</b> The CSE will review existing data about your child, including academic records, medical information (if provided), and teacher observations.</li>
            <li><b>Determination of Needed Evaluations:</b> Based on the review of existing data, the CSE will determine what additional evaluations are needed to assess your child's needs. This may include psychological evaluations, educational evaluations, speech and language evaluations, occupational therapy evaluations, and physical therapy evaluations.</li>
            <li><b>Parental Consent for Evaluations:</b> <b>The school MUST obtain your written consent before conducting any evaluations.</b>  You have the right to refuse any evaluation you do not believe is necessary. (8 NYCRR §200.5(b)(1)). Read the consent forms carefully and ask questions if you are unsure about anything. The consent form will specify the type of evaluation to be conducted and the purpose of the evaluation.</li>
            <li><b>Conducting Evaluations:</b> Once you provide consent, the school will schedule and conduct the necessary evaluations. Make sure your child attends these evaluations and is prepared to participate fully.</li>
            <li><b>Evaluation Reports:</b> You are entitled to receive copies of all evaluation reports <u>prior</u> to the CSE meeting. Request these reports well in advance of the meeting to allow yourself time to review them thoroughly.</li>
            <li><b>CSE Meeting:</b> The CSE meeting is held to review the evaluation results, discuss your child's strengths and needs, and determine eligibility for special education services.</li>
            <li><b>Eligibility Determination:</b>  Based on the evaluation results and other relevant information, the CSE will determine whether your child meets the criteria for one of the thirteen disability classifications defined by IDEA and NYS regulations.</li>
        </ol>

        <p><b>Parent Advocacy Tips for the 60-Day Timeline:</b></p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Keep Thorough Records:</b> Maintain a detailed record of all communication with the school and the CSE, including dates, times, names of individuals you spoke with, and the content of the conversations.</li>
            <li><b>Follow Up Regularly:</b>  Don't be afraid to follow up with the CSE Chairperson to inquire about the status of your child's evaluation. If you haven't heard anything within a reasonable timeframe, reach out to them to ensure the process is moving forward.</li>
            <li><b>Document Delays:</b> If you believe the 60-day timeline is not being adhered to, document the specific dates of delays and the reasons provided by the school. This documentation will be essential if you need to file a complaint.</li>
            <li><b>Seek Legal Advice:</b> If you are concerned about the timeline or believe the school is not fulfilling its obligations, consult with a special education attorney or advocate.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Understanding Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs)</h3>
        <p>As a parent, you have the right to obtain an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) of your child at public expense if you disagree with the evaluation conducted by the school district (34 CFR § 300.502). An IEE is an evaluation conducted by a qualified examiner who is not employed by the school district. If you request an IEE at public expense, the school district must either pay for the IEE or request a due process hearing to show that their evaluation was appropriate.</p>

        <p><b>Important Considerations Regarding IEEs:</b></p>
        <ul>
            <li><b>Justification for the IEE:</b> You must provide a reason for disagreeing with the school's evaluation. This reason should be specific and based on your observations of your child.</li>
            <li><b>District Criteria:</b> The school district may have specific criteria for IEEs, such as qualifications of the evaluator and maximum cost. Be sure to understand these criteria before obtaining an IEE.</li>
            <li><b>Using the IEE:</b> If you obtain an IEE at your own expense, the results of the IEE must be considered by the CSE in making decisions about your child's special education program.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Navigating District 75 (D75) Programs</h3>
        <p>For families considering District 75 (D75) programs, it's important to understand that the CSE determines the appropriate placement based on your child's individual needs. D75 programs are designed to serve students with more significant disabilities who require a more specialized and intensive level of support. The CSE will consider a variety of factors, including your child's evaluation results, academic performance, and social-emotional needs, when making placement recommendations. Parent input is also a critical factor.</p>
		<p><b>Important Considerations for D75 Placement:</b></p>
		<ul>
			<li>D75 placements are for students with the most significant needs.</li>
			<li>The CSE team must thoroughly review all evaluations before recommending a D75 placement.</li>
			<li>Parents have the right to visit proposed D75 placements before agreeing to them.</li>
		</ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h3>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h3>
        <dl>
            <dt><b>Q: What if my child attends a private school?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: Even if your child attends a private school in District 21, you are still entitled to request a special education evaluation from the NYC Department of Education. The CSE will conduct the evaluation and determine if your child is eligible for special education services. If your child is found eligible, the CSE will develop a service plan outlining the services that will be provided to your child. These services may be provided at the private school or at a designated public school site.</dd>

            <dt><b>Q: What if I disagree with the CSE's decision?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: You have the right to disagree with the CSE's decision regarding your child's eligibility for special education services, the IEP developed for your child, or the placement recommended for your child. If you disagree, you have several options, including:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Mediation:</b> Mediation is a voluntary process in which you and the school district meet with a neutral third party to try to resolve your dispute.</li>
                    <li><b>Impartial Hearing:</b> An impartial hearing is a formal legal proceeding in which an impartial hearing officer hears evidence and makes a decision about your dispute. You have the right to be represented by an attorney or advocate at an impartial hearing.</li>
                    <li><b>State Complaint:</b> You can file a complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) if you believe the school district has violated IDEA or state special education regulations.</li>
                </ul>
            </dd>

            <dt><b>Q: What are some common pitfalls parents encounter during the evaluation process?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: Some common pitfalls include:
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Lack of Documentation:</b> Failing to maintain thorough records of communication with the school and the CSE.</li>
                    <li><b>Insufficiently Detailed Referral:</b> Submitting a written referral that does not clearly articulate your concerns about your child's needs.</li>
                    <li><b>Missing Deadlines:</b> Failing to adhere to the 60-day timeline and not following up with the school to ensure the evaluation process is moving forward.</li>
                    <li><b>Feeling Intimidated:</b> Feeling intimidated by the CSE team and not actively participating in the decision-making process.</li>
                    <li><b>Not Seeking Support:</b> Not seeking support from a special education attorney or advocate.</li>
                </ul>
            </dd>

            <dt><b>Q: What if my child is bilingual or an English Language Learner (ELL)?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: Under Part 200.6 of the NYS Regulations, if your child is an English Language Learner (ELL), the evaluations must be conducted in the language and form most likely to yield accurate information on what the child knows and can do academically, developmentally, and functionally, unless it is clearly not feasible to do so. This may involve using bilingual evaluators or interpreters. You have the right to request that evaluations be conducted in your child's native language.</dd>
        </dl>
    </section>
</body>
